<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n33581" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐλελίχθ-ων</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ον</span>,
		gen. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ονος</span>, (<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐλελίζω</span> *a) <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>earth-shaking</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τετραορία</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Pi.
					<u>P.</u>
					2.4
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἐλέλιχθον</span>, i.e. Poseidon,
				ib.<i><span class="en">
				6.50
			</span></i>:—in <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>Ant.</u>
				153
			</span></i> Dionysus is called <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὁ Θήβας
			ἐ</span>. because <i>the ground shook</i> beneath the feet of
			his dancing bands.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
